{"name":"Whitewashed Fence","hex":"#faf2e3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"whitewashed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of whitewash"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Painted with the temporary paint whitewash.","Having had any controversy or potential for scandal removed, ignored or downplayed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whitewashed"},{"term":"fence","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A thin artificial barrier that separates two pieces of land or forms a perimeter enclosing the lands of a house, building, etc.","Someone who hides or buys and sells stolen goods, a criminal middleman for transactions of stolen goods."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enclose, contain or separate by building fence.","To defend or guard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fence","blocks":[{"text":"A fence is a structure that encloses an area, typically outdoors creating a boundary, and is usually constructed from posts that are connected by boards, wire, rails or netting. A fence differs from a wall in not having a solid foundation along its whole length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Westtown.jpg/330px-Westtown.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fence","wikidata":"Q148571"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Railroad_tracks_through_East_Germany_in_1977.jpg/500px-Railroad_tracks_through_East_Germany_in_1977.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Railroad_tracks_through_East_Germany_in_1977.jpg/1280px-Railroad_tracks_through_East_Germany_in_1977.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb